@!internal

--- @namespace io
module nupp.runtime.standardio

--[[
`io` is bytes: storage for them, and the readers and writers that move
them. Reach for it when a program needs a growable buffer, an immutable
snapshot of one, or a parser that works the same over a buffer, a file and an
HTTP response body.

```nupp:playground
local bytes = io.newbuffer("hello")
local reader = bytes:newReader()
assert(reader:read(5) == "hello")
```

A buffer holds its bytes in the target's physical storage: a native array on
LuaJIT, or a rooted allocation in a Wasm application. `Reader` and `Writer` are
interfaces rather than the concrete things that satisfy them. Code written
against either contract works over every implementation without knowing which
one it has.

The remaining I/O operations have their own modules. See [](io.path) for filesystem names,
[](io.uri) for resource identifiers, [](io.files) for the filesystem
itself, [](nupp.io.process) for running a child process, [](io.net) for
network connections, [](io.tls) for encrypting one, and [](io.http)
for the asynchronous HTTP client.
Keeping them apart is what lets a program that wants a byte buffer carry a byte
buffer rather than every provider behind the name.

Files, processes, network connections, TLS sessions, and HTTP bodies move bytes
through the `Reader` and `Writer` contracts on this page. A parser written
against byte I/O therefore accepts all of them.

Buffers, views, readers, writers, and their adapters are owners. A factory that
takes a reader or writer transfers its close obligation to the adapter; a
factory that borrows a buffer or byte queue leaves that value with its caller.
`close` consumes an owner. Types that expose `isReleased` report their state
without changing it.

## Buffers

`newbuffer()` creates an empty growable buffer. A string supplies initial
bytes; an integer reserves capacity without changing the length.

```nupp
local bytes = io.newbuffer("hello")
bytes:setString("!", bytes:len())
assert(bytes:getString() == "hello!")
```

Buffer offsets are zero-based, as they are wherever a count is an offset into
storage rather than a position in a Lua string. See [Byte
positions](docs/learn/runtime/data/standard-library.md#byte-positions) for the
rule and where the other convention applies.

`getString(offset, count)` copies a range. `setString` overwrites from an
offset and grows the buffer as needed, filling any gap with zero bytes. `clear`
sets the length to zero without discarding capacity, and `resize` truncates or
zero-fills:

```nupp
local bytes = io.newbuffer("hello")
bytes:ensureCapacity(4096)
assert(bytes:capacity() >= 4096)
bytes:resize(3)
assert(bytes:getString() == "hel")
```

Capacity is the allocation, not a recorded number. Growing at least doubles it,
so appending through a writer costs amortized constant time per byte and
`capacity()` reports bytes that are actually held. `ensureCapacity` reserves at
least the minimum asked for.

`close()` releases the buffer's storage and returns nothing, and an operation on
a released buffer raises rather than answering a reason.

`readSpan()` borrows the buffer's initialized bytes without separating its
pointer from its length. Native producers write through `reserveWrite(offset,
count)`: the returned lease supplies a checked writable span, and
`commit(written)` changes the logical length only after the producer succeeds.
Dropping the lease aborts it and leaves the length unchanged.

## Byte views

`view(offset, count)` returns an immutable snapshot, not a mutable alias into
the buffer. It remains valid if the source buffer changes or closes:

```nupp
local buffer = io.newbuffer("header-body")
local header = buffer:view(0, 6)
buffer:clear()
assert(header:getString() == "header")
header:close()
```

A view can make a smaller view, open its own snapshot reader, report its byte
length, copy to a string, borrow a checked span with `readSpan()`, and be
closed. Its offsets are zero-based like a buffer's. The data and UTF-8 modules
accept views, which is what keeps those APIs from being coupled to a mutable
buffer.

## Readers

A `io.Reader` is a forward-only byte source. `newstringreader(text)` reads
a string; `buffer:newReader()` reads a snapshot of the buffer's current
contents. A reader owns its close obligation and closes automatically at the
end of its scope unless `close()` consumes it earlier.

```nupp
local reader = io.newstringreader("abcdef")
assert(reader:read(2) == "ab")

local destination = io.newbuffer()
assert(reader:readInto(destination, 0, 3) == 3)
assert(destination:getString() == "cde")
assert(reader:read(8) == "f")
assert(reader:read(8) == "") -- EOF
```

`read(count)` requires a positive count and returns at most that many bytes. It
returns an empty string at EOF and nil with a reason after close.
`readSpan(writable)` fills a positive-sized checked writable span.
`readInto(buffer, offset, count)` returns zero at EOF and reads 64 KiB when
given no count; an explicit count must be positive. A failed or EOF read does
not extend the destination. `transferTo(writer)` copies the entire remaining
source and returns the byte count.

A read moves the reader's cursor as a write moves a writer's, so all four take
the reader exclusively. Two readers over one cursor, such as a stream and a
borrowed view of it, cannot both be reading at once; the checker refuses the
second while the first is live.

`readall(reader, maxBytes)` takes a reader and returns everything left in it as
one string, closing the reader on the way out. A limit is checked as the bytes
arrive, so a source larger than it returns a reason rather than a string:

```nupp
assert(io.readall(io.newstringreader("whole")) == "whole")
local text, reason = io.readall(io.newstringreader("too long"), 3)
assert(text == nil and reason ~= nil)
```

## Writers

`buffer:newWriter()` clears the buffer and returns a forward-only writer
targeting it. A writer likewise closes automatically; `flush()` publishes
pending work without consuming that ownership. The writer holds its buffer
exclusively for as long as it is live, so the buffer is read once the writer is
gone:

```nupp
local destination = io.newbuffer()
do
    local writer = destination:newWriter()
    assert(writer:write("prefix:"))

    local payload = io.newbuffer("body")
    assert(writer:writeSpan(payload:readSpan()) == 4)
    assert(writer:flush())
end
assert(destination:getString() == "prefix:body")
```

`write` returns a boolean and `writeSpan` returns the byte count. Both return a
reason when the writer is closed or the destination was released. The span is
the common zero-copy input contract for buffers, views, files, processes, and
HTTP transfers; there is no unchecked raw-pointer writer alongside it. `flush`
does nothing for memory. It is part of the contract because a file or socket
buffers, and `flush` is where one reports a write that failed to land: `close`
returns nothing, so a caller that has to know flushes first.

## Lines

`newlines(reader, limit)` takes a reader and separates it at `\n` and `\r\n`.
The terminator is not returned, and a final fragment before EOF is still a
line:

```nupp
local lines = io.newlines(io.newstringreader("one\r\ntwo\nlast"))
assert(lines:read() == "one")
assert(lines:read() == "two")
assert(lines:read() == "last")
assert(lines:read() == nil) -- EOF
```

The default limit is 64 KiB. An overlong line or source failure returns nil
with a reason. Closing or dropping the line reader closes the source it took.

## Typed scalars

A reader and a writer move bytes. `newscalarreader` and `newscalarwriter` add
the missing piece, a sized integer or float landing on those bytes without an
`ffi.cast` at every call site.

```nupp
local writer = io.newscalarwriter()
writer:writeUint32(1447383632):writeFloat64(1.5)

local stored = writer:buffer()
assert(stored ~= nil)
local reader = io.newscalarreader(stored)
assert(reader:readUint32() == 1447383632)
assert(reader:readFloat64() == 1.5)
assert(reader:atEnd())
```

Every write returns the writer, so calls chain. A short read raises rather than
answering a reason, which is the one place this pair departs from the reader and
writer contracts above: a scalar either landed whole or the source was not what
the format said, and there is no partial value to hand back.

::: deepdive Host byte order
Both directions read and write host-endian, the only order LuaJIT ships, so
that is the default. A format fixed to one byte order casts and swaps
explicitly, and there are no LE and BE variants here until something needs
them. Adding a second set without a caller would double the surface and leave
half of it untested.
:::

### Scalar sources

`newscalarreader` takes whatever holds the bytes. A string, a `ByteView` or a
`Buffer` is read from a copy taken there and then, so `remaining()` knows the
count:

```nupp
local reader = io.newscalarreader("\1\2\3\4")
assert(reader:remaining() == 4)
```

A `Reader` is consumed as it goes, which is what lets a file or an HTTP body be
read a field at a time. It cannot say how much is left, so `remaining()` returns
nil and `atEnd()` is the question to ask instead. The scalar adapter takes that
reader: closing the adapter, by hand or at the end of its scope, closes the
reader exactly once.

### Scalar destinations

`newscalarwriter` appends to a `Buffer`, keeping what that buffer already
holds, or writes through a `Writer`. Given nothing it starts a buffer of its
own, which `buffer()` hands back:

```nupp
local destination = io.newbuffer("header:")
do
    local scalars = io.newscalarwriter(destination)
    scalars:writeUint8(33)
end
assert(destination:getString() == "header:!")
```

A writer pointed at somebody else's `Writer` returns nil from `buffer()`, since
the bytes are already gone. That writer is taken, so closing the scalar adapter
closes it exactly once. A buffer supplied by the caller is only borrowed. The
buffer created by the no-argument form belongs to the scalar writer; `buffer()`
borrows it for as long as the scalar writer remains live.

## Failures

The files, process, network, TLS and HTTP modules return a failure from the
environment as `nil, err`, where `err` is a [](io.Error): a `kind` a
program can branch on and a `message` a person can read. The kind is the same on
every platform and provider, so a missing file is `"notFound"` whether the
operating system or the browser said so; the message is whatever the platform
said and is not.

```nupp
local text, err = io.files.read("settings.json")
if text == nil and err ~= nil and err.kind == "notFound" then
    text = "{}"
end
```

An error prints as its message, and joins text the same way, so
`assert(files.read(path))` and `print("cannot read: " .. err)` read as they
would with a string. The reads and writes of the `Reader` and `Writer` contracts
are the exception: their reason stays a string, the same message, because a
buffer or a string reader returns through the same contract.

## Byte queues

A [`nupp.text`](nupp.text) buffer is a byte queue: `put` appends to the back
and `get` consumes from the front. `newscalarreader` accepts one directly, so
bytes assembled there are read without copying everything in it first, and the
queue's own `get` stays usable over the same bytes.

```nupp
local queue = nupp.text.newbuffer()
queue:put("header:")
queue:put("!")

local reader = io.newscalarreader(queue)
assert(reader:readBytes(7) == "header:")
assert(queue:tostring() == "!")
assert(reader:readUint8() == 33)
```

Reading drains the queue, and `remaining()` reports what it still holds.
`newqueuereader` is the same bridge one layer down: it returns an ordinary
`Reader` over the queue, for `transferTo`, `readInto`, and everything else that
contract already covers.

```nupp
local destination = io.newbuffer()
local queue = nupp.text.newbuffer()
queue:put("payload")
do
    local reader = io.newqueuereader(queue)
    assert(reader:transferTo(destination:newWriter()) == 7)
end
assert(destination:getString() == "payload")
```

Neither takes the queue over. Nothing here frees or closes it, and one read to
empty leaves an empty queue rather than a released one.

::: seealso
- [](io.files) for the same contracts over a file on disk
- [](io.path) for filesystem names rather than file contents
- [ownership.md](docs/learn/runtime/ownership/borrowing.md) for the complete
  contract every owned value here follows
- [standard-library.md](docs/learn/runtime/data/standard-library.md) for the
  errors, ownership, and byte-position conventions every facility shares
:::
]]

local source1 = require("nupp.io")
export type Buffer = source1.Buffer
export type BufferWriteLease = source1.BufferWriteLease
export type ByteQueue = source1.ByteQueue
export type ByteView = source1.ByteView
export type Error = source1.Error
export type ErrorKind = source1.ErrorKind
export type Lines = source1.Lines
export type Reader = source1.Reader
export type ScalarReader = source1.ScalarReader
export type ScalarWriter = source1.ScalarWriter
export type Writer = source1.Writer
export const Error = source1.Error
export const newbuffer = source1.newBuffer
export const newlines = source1.newLines
export const newqueuereader = source1.newQueueReader
export const newscalarreader = source1.newScalarReader
export const newscalarwriter = source1.newScalarWriter
export const newstringreader = source1.newStringReader
export const readall = source1.readAll

--- @internal
export const value: table = do
    local original = rawget(_G, "io") as any
    original.Error = Error
    original.newbuffer = newbuffer
    original.newlines = newlines
    original.newqueuereader = newqueuereader
    original.newscalarreader = newscalarreader
    original.newscalarwriter = newscalarwriter
    original.newstringreader = newstringreader
    original.readall = readall
    yield original
end
